  "name": "Question 17",
  "problem": "In a factory, cans are filled with soda. The amount of soda in one can is normally distributed with a mean of $340\\,\\text{g}$ and a variance of $4\\,{\\text{g}}^{2}.$ The weight of a can is normally distributed with a mean of $25\\,\\text{g}$ and a variance of $1\\,{\\text{g}}^{2}$ If one can containing soda is randomly selected, what is the probability that it weighs more than $360\\,\\text{g}?$ You may assume that the weights of soda and cans are independent.\n\n*Hint: A table of values of $Φ(z)$ for different values of $z$ is given below, where $Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution. *\n\n| $z$ | $\\,0.02\\,$ | $\\,0.03\\,$ | $\\,0.04\\,$ | $\\,0.05\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,-2.3\\,$ | $\\,0.0102\\,$ | $\\,0.0099\\,$ | $\\,0.0096\\,$ | $\\,0.0094\\,$ |\n| $\\,-2.2\\,$ | $\\,0.0132\\,$ | $\\,0.0129\\,$ | $\\,0.0125\\,$ | $\\,0.0122\\,$ |\n| $\\,-2.1\\,$ | $\\,0.0170\\,$ | $\\,0.0166\\,$ | $\\,0.0162\\,$ | $\\,0.0158\\,$ |",
